WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.449
People are freaked out by Spiders.

00:00:02.449 --> 00:00:08.660
It's not just that they have so many disproportionately
long legs or move erratically it's also that

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:13.849
they have fangs that can bite you, and they
kill things wayyy bigger than they are.

00:00:13.849 --> 00:00:15.969
Yeah, real spiders are scary.

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:19.720
But how about a giant spider that could actually
eat you?

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:25.640
Meet the jorōgumo: taking the scary spider
thing to a whole other level for centuries.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:30.350
Appearing as an attractive woman to lure her
victims to a painful death, this giant shape

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:36.309
shifting spider of Japanese folklore is both
beautiful and deadly—like the real-world

00:00:36.309 --> 00:00:37.920
spider that shares her name.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:43.399
For now let’s explore how this deadly female
monster snared imagination in her web by taking

00:00:43.399 --> 00:00:49.469
a look at ancient storytelling and the mysterious
world of the Japanese yōkai

00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:53.719
[Monstrum intro.]

00:00:53.719 --> 00:00:59.679
Broadly speaking the word yōkai refers to
any weird or supernatural creature or phenomena

00:00:59.679 --> 00:01:01.449
in Japanese folklore.

00:01:01.449 --> 00:01:07.360
Yōkai offer explanations for things that
seem unusual or defy the known world—they

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.660
are personal and cultural—shifting meaning
or appearance based on who is telling the

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.540
story and for what purpose.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:20.250
A yōkai can be pretty much anything strange,
from oddly moving smoke [enenra],to head-eating

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.380
spirits [kubikajiri] or even a mischievous
paper umbrella [karakasa kozō].

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:31.660
The word yōkai is made from two Chinese characters,
kanji, that both indicate strangeness or mystery,

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:36.730
and the word appears in Japanese text at least
as early as the eighth century [in Shoku Nihongi].

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:41.610
The term popped up more frequently in the
mid-Edo period but wasn’t really popularized

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:46.080
as the term for anything unexplainable until
the Meiji period (1868-1912).

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.030
A 20th-century Japanese folklorist [Yanagita
Kunio] solidified its place in culture when

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:53.160
he began using it in his academic writing.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:58.600
Before yōkai became the common term for all
things creepy, spooky, and strange in Japan,

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.070
there were a whole slew of names.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:05.090
The name changed based on time period and
cultural popularity.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:10.479
In the Heian period “oni” referred to
anything threatening although most often that

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:14.160
thing was a demon or ogre that had a humanlike
shape.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.239
During the Edo period, another word could
be used to label any shapeshifting being—

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:22.379
or any oddly formed or scary creature.

00:02:22.379 --> 00:02:26.110
So the jorōgumo falls under any of these
categories [yokai, oni, bakemono] since it

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:32.040
is a strange, threatening creature that can
take on a human shape or a spider form.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.040
Covering all the bases with all those legs
I guess.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.530
Jorōgumo first appeared in literature in
the Edo period.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:43.389
It is highly likely that they previously existed
as part of an oral folklore tradition.

00:02:43.389 --> 00:02:50.770
The jorō in jorōgumo , translates to prostitute,
but is also associated with the word jōrō,

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:55.550
which historically referred to the women who
waited on female royalty.

00:02:55.550 --> 00:03:00.099
Also known as the “binding bride,” “whore
spider,” “entangling bride,” and “harlot

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:05.110
spider,” this shapeshifting creature is
unsurprisingly known for her ability to lure

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:10.859
handsome men into her clutches by disguising
herself as a beautiful woman before using

00:03:10.859 --> 00:03:14.239
a painful venom to kill her victims slowly.

00:03:14.239 --> 00:03:18.400
In some stories, this venom gives the monster
the ability to shapeshift.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:23.190
The jorōgumo is said to spin silk strong
enough to capture a man in her web and she

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.720
can control other spiders.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:30.510
Illustrations sometimes show this creature
as a woman with spider legs, or just as a

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.159
large spider.

00:03:32.159 --> 00:03:35.489
Spiders play a central role in a lot of Japanese
folklore.

00:03:35.489 --> 00:03:40.849
The earliest known surviving Japanese text
depicting a monstrous spider, a picture scroll

00:03:40.849 --> 00:03:46.489
from the 14th century, includes a story about
a shape-shifting Earth spider that first appears

00:03:46.489 --> 00:03:49.319
[see page 80 of Reider text, available online]
to two warriors as a beautiful woman.

00:03:49.319 --> 00:03:54.639
She tries to trap them by throwing balls of
silk webbing before they stab her and she

00:03:54.639 --> 00:03:55.719
flees.

00:03:55.719 --> 00:04:02.099
Later the men find a many-legged creature
wounded in the same area the woman was stabbed.

00:04:02.099 --> 00:04:08.150
They cut the creature's abdomen open and almost
two thousand human heads fall out along with

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:11.129
spiders the size of small children [page 83].

00:04:11.129 --> 00:04:15.519
The creature is called a [yama-goom-o] or
mountain spider in the text.

00:04:15.519 --> 00:04:21.400
This seems awfully similar to the jorōgumo
and it definitely shows the association between

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:23.460
and spiders in Japanese folklore.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:29.160
One of the earliest written stories of the
jorogumo comes from a 17th-century text.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:34.290
It tells of a young samurai who takes shelter
in an abandoned structure strung with spider

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:35.290
webs.

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:40.479
He is approached by a young woman carrying
a child who, she insists, was fathered by

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:41.479
him.

00:04:41.479 --> 00:04:47.060
The man is instantly suspicious, since the
woman was traveling alone at night in a remote

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:48.060
place.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:53.840
The child goes to approach the samurai many
times, but turns away each time when it sees

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:55.310
the man’s sword.

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:59.390
Annoyed and impatient, the man slashes the
woman with his blade.

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:01.380
She flees to the rafters of the structure.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:07.930
The next morning, the samurai finds an attic
where a giant two-foot long spider lays, dying

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:13.330
from deep cuts along it’s back—exactly
like the ones he gave the young woman.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:16.879
A stone grave statue of a young child is there
as well.

00:05:16.879 --> 00:05:23.190
The samurai looks up—and sees a horrific
sight –carcasses of all the spider-woman’s

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.789
victims, wrapped in her web.

00:05:25.789 --> 00:05:28.511
Another tale from an 18th-century collection
of ghost stories [Taihei Hyakumonogatari]

00:05:28.511 --> 00:05:29.949
has a different twist.

00:05:29.949 --> 00:05:35.449
One hot summer day, Magoroku, the heir of
a wealthy samurai family, relaxes and sings

00:05:35.449 --> 00:05:36.449
on his porch.

00:05:36.449 --> 00:05:40.889
Suddenly, a woman, who appears to be about
50-years-old, approaches him.

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:45.490
She tells him that his singing is so beautiful,
it’s making her daughter fall in love with

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:48.280
him, so much so that she wants to marry him.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:54.370
Curious to know if what the woman says is
true, he follows her to a large mansion.

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:59.949
Inside, he finds a beautiful young woman,
who begs Magoroku to consider her as his bride.

00:05:59.949 --> 00:06:04.069
Magoroku declines her offer, because he is
in fact already wed.

00:06:04.069 --> 00:06:09.460
Both the young woman and her mother beg him
to reconsider but he remains resolute in his

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:11.340
devotion to his wife.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:16.259
So he runs from the mansion, but the second
he steps outside the door it mysteriously

00:06:16.259 --> 00:06:20.830
vanishes, turning back into the bamboo surrounding
his own house.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:25.699
His servants insist that he has been on the
porch sleeping the entire time, but Magoroku

00:06:25.699 --> 00:06:29.720
cannot believe that what he experienced was
only a dream.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:33.199
Suddenly, he sees a female spider scuttling
away.

00:06:33.199 --> 00:06:39.100
Looking around he realizes his home is strung
with numerous spider webs, even though earlier,

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:42.210
he had driven the spiders away with his pipe
smoke.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:47.460
Then he remembered how the beautiful girl
told him the older woman had also been driven

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:48.460
away by smoking.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:49.460
Magoroku realizes, to his horror, that the
spiders were taking revenge on him.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:53.620
He orders the servants to remove the webs,
and fortunately for Magoroku, the jorogumo

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:55.349
never comes to him again.

00:06:55.349 --> 00:07:00.810
An attempted revenge via bigamy and a trick
to lure [loo-ur] a man to his death?

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:04.319
Not all yōkai are monsters, but the jorogumo
certainly is.

00:07:04.319 --> 00:07:06.020
But why spiders at all?

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:10.490
You could argue that part of the association
comes from the cultural idea that women are

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:15.830
out to “ensnare” men with their physical
or sexual appeal or because of the construction

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:17.710
of the spider web itself.

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:25.169
Weaving, spinning, and other elements of textile
production were historically areas women dominated.

00:07:25.169 --> 00:07:29.939
Silk weaving in particular, was an important
cultural, religious, and economic activity

00:07:29.939 --> 00:07:30.939
in Japan.

00:07:30.939 --> 00:07:36.800
Then there’s the real life “Joro spider”
or “Jorō-gumo spider” a member of the

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:37.979
Nephila genus.

00:07:37.979 --> 00:07:42.639
Commonly called a “golden orb-weaver”
in English, with their large size and unique,

00:07:42.639 --> 00:07:47.990
yellow colored silk and webs, it’s unsurprising
that in Japan these spiders are said to have

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:50.110
supernatural powers.

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:55.210
Some tales say that once these spiders reach
400 years of age, they develop magical powers

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:57.300
and can feed on humans.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.870
They also can shapeshift into beautiful, young
women.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:06.849
Interestingly, female golden-orb weaver spiders
are much larger than their drab, brown-colored

00:08:06.849 --> 00:08:08.280
male counterparts.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:12.879
The females are yellow, red, and black and
can be up to four times larger than the males

00:08:12.879 --> 00:08:17.479
of the species, reaching 3 to 4 inch [7.6-10
centimeters) wide leg spans.

00:08:17.479 --> 00:08:21.930
Their golden webs can be large enough to even
trap small birds and bats.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:26.669
Given the name of these spiders and how closely
its legends relate to the yokai, my guess

00:08:26.669 --> 00:08:32.279
is that the real golden orb-weaver spider
helped inspire the original oral folklore

00:08:32.279 --> 00:08:33.860
of the jorōgumo.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.909
Scary lady spider equals scary spider lady.

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:43.310
The East Asian golden-orb weaver spiders were
first discovered in North America in 2014,

00:08:43.310 --> 00:08:47.980
which seems ironic given that their fictional
counterpart is becoming increasingly popular

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:50.430
here and in other parts of the globe.

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:55.450
While long associated with folklore in Japan,
yokai have left a strong literary and visual

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:56.450
history.

00:08:56.450 --> 00:09:00.350
When illustrated scrolls became popular during
the Classical Heian period (c. 794-1185),

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:05.010
yōkai that largely existed as oral stories
took on visual form.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:10.620
By recording these unusual beings, their forms
became more solidified in how they were portrayed,

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.649
but the art inspired many other different
kinds of yōkai.

00:09:13.649 --> 00:09:19.190
Today, we’ve seen a similar resurgence as
the jorogumo becomes more well-known outside

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:20.190
of Japan.

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:26.339
The internet and a world that’s more connected
than ever made manga and anime more popular

00:09:26.339 --> 00:09:31.070
outside of Japan, and the jorōgumo pops up
in both of these.

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:35.720
I’m always fascinated by monsters that are
exclusively female, and at least with the

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:41.310
jorōgumo there might be a good reason, a
reason seemingly based on a real animal.

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:46.320
Cultural and spiritual beliefs, mixed together
with the natural world, weave together a story

00:09:46.320 --> 00:10:01.540
that is completely unique—and absolutely
terrifying.

